// PromiseType.maxon — everything the compiler knows about the STORAGE TYPE `Promise with T` /
// `Promise with (T, E)` (P1.5-B2c). One home, because the same three facts are read by the type index
// (is this slot managed?), the parser's storage door (may this spawn be stored here?) and the await
// (what does this promise return, and what does it throw?), and a disagreement between any two of them
// is a wrong answer rather than a diagnostic.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`Promise` IS A DECLARED STDLIB TYPE, NOT A COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED BASE.** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`
// writes `type Promise uses Element, ErrorType` with a single field, so the `with` grammar, the alias
// registry, the instance interner and the arity check all serve it already. Nothing here re-derives any of
// that; what it adds is the three facts a DECLARATION cannot state.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE FIRST FACT: A PROMISE HANDLE IS NOT A REFCOUNTED HEAP RECORD, SO A `Promise` INSTANCE IS A
// TRIVIAL ONE-WORD SLOT.** `Parser.decrefCalleeFor` has said this about the VALUE since B2 — *"a promise
// owns no `__mm_alloc` box; the handle is the GT struct pointer"* — and B2c is where the TYPE learns to say
// it too. `ProgramSignatures.typeIsManaged` answers `true` for every other generic instance because every
// other one IS a base-struct box; this one's runtime value is a pointer into the scheduler's own slab,
// whose lifetime is the green thread's and is discharged by `__gt_promise_drop` at the parse-tracked sites,
// never by a refcount.
//
// ⛔ **AND THAT IS A DEPARTURE FROM THE BOOTSTRAP, WHICH BOXES.** `maxon-sharp`'s `BoxPromiseIntoStruct`
// allocates a real `Promise` record at the storage site and `ReconstructPromiseFromStruct` loads `inner`
// back out at the await. shv2 stores the HANDLE ITSELF, typed at the instance, and the reasons are two and
// both measured:
//
//   • **A BOX WOULD HAVE BEEN UNREACHABLE HERE WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN, AND IS NOT ANY MORE — the argument
//     rests on the SECOND bullet now.** A boxed `Promise` element makes `Array with IntPromise` a
//     MANAGED-element array, and shv2's managed-element array requires its element to be deep-cloneable as
//     a single `(box) -> newBox` function (`ProgramSignatures.typeSupportsDeepClone`). A non-`Array` generic
//     instance was not, so `Parser.requireOpaqueArrayCopyable` refused it with E2015 — MEASURED on a stand-in
//     (`type Cell uses Slot` with one `int` field): `push` and `get` compile, and `for c in arr` was **E2015
//     raised inside `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `slice`**; five of this spec's storage cases iterate. **W162 built
//     that per-instance cloner**, so this refusal no longer stands in the way and the box is merely wrong
//     rather than impossible.
//   • **AND CLONING A PROMISE IS WRONG ANYWAY.** Two boxes naming one green thread is the double free
//     `await`'s linearity (E3100) exists to make unrepresentable, so a cloner for this type would be a
//     mechanism whose every use is a bug.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "NOTHING OBSERVABLE DEPENDS ON THE BOX", AND THAT WAS A SILENT WRONG
// ANSWER FOR AS LONG AS IT STOOD (G17).** Its premise was right — `Promise.inner` is documented in
// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:124` as *"a raw GT pointer"*, which is exactly what the unboxed value IS, and a box
// would be an allocation the leak gate has to see freed, which storing the handle never makes. What did not
// follow is the conclusion, because **NOTHING HAD TOLD THE FIELD-READ DOOR**: the ordinary lowering of a
// field read is *load the word at the field's offset*, and with the value being the handle that
// DEREFERENCES it. `inner` is at offset 0, where a green thread keeps its saved stack pointer (`GtOffSp`).
// MEASURED on `function peek(p IntPromise) returns Integer { return p.inner }`:
// `x64.loadRegBaseDisp.word64 r8, [rcx + 0]` — `gt->sp`, handed to a caller that asked for the handle. It
// never faulted (the stack is committed) and never crashed; it answered a plausible number, and
// `Testing/SpecWorkerPool.drainHasAnswered` would have peeked a word 16 bytes into the green thread's own
// stack. ⇒ `fieldReadIsThePromiseHandle` below is what the door now asks, and `specs-shv2/promise-peek.md`
// is what would have caught it: with the read left as a load, its `completes-under-the-drive` case exits
// **0xC0000005**.
//
// ⭐ **THE SECOND FACT: THE ERROR TYPE IS AN ARGUMENT, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE NON-THROWING CASE.** The
// declaration names two parameters and `Promise with T` supplies one. That is not an arity error — the
// omission is the SPELLING of "this thunk does not throw", which is why the trailing parameter is optional
// (`promiseOptionalTrailingTypeParams`) rather than defaulted to something. `maxon-sharp` spells the same
// rule as `OptionalTrailingTypeParamCount`, and neither compiler can read it off the declaration because
// the language has no syntax for an optional type parameter.
//
// ⭐ **THE THIRD FACT: A STORED PROMISE IS UNOWNED BY THE FRAME THAT READS IT.** `specs/async-await.md`'s
// *"Known boundary"* paragraph states it: a promise that escapes into storage is not tracked, and awaiting
// it twice still double-frees at run time. So a value read back out of a container is a promise for the
// purposes of `await` (it answers `Parser.valueIsPromise`) and is NOT one for the purposes of ownership
// (it never answers `Parser.valueOwnsGreenThread`). The two questions are separate doors in the parser for
// exactly this reason.

// The declared base name. The ONE spelling of it — every predicate below and
// `ProgramSignatures.instanceIsPromiseHandle` compare against this rather than against their own literal.
export let PromiseTypeName = b"Promise"

// ⭐⭐ **THE STAND-IN RESULT ARGUMENT FOR A VOID THUNK'S PROMISE.** `async voidFn()` is a promise like
// any other and must carry a TYPE, but `MaxonType` has no void arm to put in the result position — void is
// a property of a RETURN, spelled by `MaxonReturnType.void`, and a type argument position has nowhere to
// say it. So a void thunk's promise interns `Promise with __Void`, on the precedent of
// `SynthesizedByteElementName` (`b"__ManagedByte"`): a compiler-minted type NAME standing where a source
// type would.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY UNSPELLABLE.** E2051 reserves the `__` prefix for the compiler, and
// `checkGenericArgType` runs only over arguments an author actually wrote, so no program can name this and
// no program can be shown it — `awaitedPromiseFacts` answers `MaxonReturnType.void` for such an instance
// and the await yields nothing, exactly as it did when the handle was untyped.
//
// ⚠ **A ONE-ARGUMENT INSTANCE WOULD NOT DO, and that is why this exists at all rather than just
// omitting the argument.** `promiseStorageThrows` keys on the argument COUNT: `Promise with (T, E)` throws
// and `Promise with T` does not. A void THROWING thunk that interned a single error argument would read as
// a non-throwing promise of an error-typed result — so the result position is always filled, and `__Void`
// is what fills it when there is no result.
export let PromiseVoidResultName = b"__Void"

// The one member a promise carries — `p.cancel()`. `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declares no methods on `Promise`
// (its `inner` field is the whole of its written surface), so this name is the compiler's and lives with the
// rest of what the compiler knows about the type rather than in a roster it is not on.
export let PromiseCancelMethodName = b"cancel"

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE FIELD A PROMISE CARRIES — `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:124`'s `public let inner`**, and the ONE
// spelling of its name. That declaration says what it is for in so many words: *"`inner` is exported
// (read-only via `let`) so non-blocking pollers like the spec test dispatcher can peek at the underlying
// GT's status"* — which is `__Builtins.gtIsComplete(promise.inner)`, and the whole reason the read below
// has to be right.
let PromiseInnerFieldName = b"inner"

// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS FIELD READ THE PROMISE'S OWN HANDLE RATHER THAN A LOAD?** The FIRST fact above says a
// promise's runtime value IS the green-thread pointer, so `p.inner` is the identity — exactly as a fused
// wrapper's inline `managed` is (`StructLayout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord`, whose arm in `emitFieldLoad`
// this one sits beside): in both cases the record IS the field, and a `loadIndirect` at the field's offset
// reads through a pointer the caller wanted the VALUE of.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH CONJUNCTS MATTER, for `fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord`'s reason one type over.** The type test
// alone would be right today only because `Promise` happens to declare exactly one field; the index test
// alone is a bare `== 0` about any struct. `indexOfField` answers `fieldCount()` when the name is absent,
// so a layout that somehow lacked `inner` compares unequal to every valid index rather than matching 0.
//
// ⚠ **THE RECEIVER'S TYPE IS WHAT IS ASKED, NOT THE LAYOUT'S NAME.** `typeIsPromiseStorage` routes
// through `ProgramSignatures.instanceIsPromiseHandle`, the one place the base name is compared — so this
// door, the type index and the await cannot come to disagree about which values are promises, which is the
// obligation this file's header opens with.
export function fieldReadIsThePromiseHandle(signatures ProgramSignatures, receiverType MaxonType, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns bool
	if not typeIsPromiseStorage(signatures, t: receiverType) 'notAPromise'
		return false
	end 'notAPromise'

	return index == layout.indexOfField(PromiseInnerFieldName)
end 'fieldReadIsThePromiseHandle'

// `Element` — the type the thunk RETURNS, and what an `await` of this promise yields.
export let PromiseResultArgPosition = 0 as TypeParamPosition

// `ErrorType` — the type the thunk THROWS. Present iff the storage was written `Promise with (T, E)`.
let PromiseErrorArgPosition = 1 as TypeParamPosition

// The argument count of a THROWING promise storage type. `Promise with (T, E)` ⇒ 2, `Promise with T` ⇒ 1,
// and there is no third shape — the arity check below admits exactly these two.
let PromiseThrowingArgCount = 2 as FieldIndex

// ⭐ **HOW MANY TRAILING TYPE ARGUMENTS THIS BASE MAY OMIT.** Zero for every declared generic in the tree
// but one. It is a function of the base NAME and not a field on `StructLayout` because there is exactly one
// such base and a field would be a column every layout carries to answer for one of them; if a second ever
// wants it, this is the one place that has to learn the name.
export function promiseOptionalTrailingTypeParams(baseName ByteArray) returns FieldIndex
	return 1 as FieldIndex if baseName.equals(PromiseTypeName) else 0 as FieldIndex
end 'promiseOptionalTrailingTypeParams'

// Is `t` the type of a stored promise — a `genericInstance` over the `Promise` base?
//
// ⚠ Asked of the TYPE and never of a name: `typealias StrPromise = Promise with String` means the spelling
// at a use site is `StrPromise`, and only the instance it resolves to carries the base.
export function typeIsPromiseStorage(signatures ProgramSignatures, t MaxonType) returns bool
	if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAnInstance'
		return false
	end 'notAnInstance'

	return signatures.instanceIsPromiseHandle(typeNameIdOf(t))
end 'typeIsPromiseStorage'

// ⭐ **WHAT A PROMISE STORED IN INSTANCE `gid` THROWS — read off the TYPE, which is the whole point of
// naming the error type in it.** A one-argument instance throws nothing; a two-argument one throws the
// enum its second argument names.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME COMES FROM `instanceArgDisplayName`, THE SOURCE SPELLING**, because that is the currency a
// `ThrowsClause` holds everywhere else: `FuncSignature`'s clause carries the name the author wrote after
// `throws`, and E3059 compares two such names with `.equals`. A compiled or mangled spelling here would
// report a difference in RENDERING as a difference in type — the defect `instanceArgDisplayName`'s own
// header records for E3125.
export function promiseStorageThrows(signatures ProgramSignatures, gid GenericInstanceId) returns ThrowsClause
	if signatures.genericInstanceArgCount(gid) < PromiseThrowingArgCount 'resultOnly'
		return ThrowsClause.none
	end 'resultOnly'

	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(gid, position: PromiseErrorArgPosition)))
end 'promiseStorageThrows'
